public class MinTrackingStack {
    private static class Node {
        int value;
        int minSoFar;  // minimum value from bottom to this node

        Node(int value, int minSoFar) {
            this.value = value;
            this.minSoFar = minSoFar;
        }
    }

    private Node top;

    /**
     * Push value onto stack. O(1).
     */
    public void push(int value) {
        int newMin = (top == null) ? value : Math.min(value, top.minSoFar);
        top = new Node(value, newMin);
    }

    /**
     * Pop and return top value. O(1).
     */
    public int pop() {
        if (top == null) throw new EmptyStackException();
        int result = top.value;
        top = top.next;  // simplified; link Node in real impl
        return result;
    }

    /**
     * Query: return minimum value currently in stack. O(1).
     * This is the "surprise" feature — useful for interview problems,
     * competitive programming, and priority-like behavior without
     * the overhead of a heap.
     */
    public int getMin() {
        if (top == null) throw new EmptyStackException();
        return top.minSoFar;
    }
}
```

**Why `getMin`?** It's the most algorithmically interesting constant-time stack query — you can't compute it naively from the stack's current state. It requires augmenting the data structure at push-time. It unlocks problems like "largest rectangle in histogram" and "trapping rain water" that would otherwise need O(n) auxiliary space or O(n log n) time.

The internal storage trick: each node memoizes the minimum value seen from the stack's bottom through that node. When you pop, that information naturally leaves with the node. When you push, compute the new minimum in O(1) by comparing the incoming value against the previous top's cached minimum.

