public class MinStackWithSum {
    private static class Node {
        int value;
        int minBelowOrEqual;
        long sumBelowOrEqual;

        Node(int value, int minBelowOrEqual, long sumBelowOrEqual) {
            this.value = value;
            this.minBelowOrEqual = minBelowOrEqual;
            this.sumBelowOrEqual = sumBelowOrEqual;
        }
    }

    private Node[] stack;
    private int size;

    public MinStackWithSum(int initialCapacity) {
        stack = new Node[initialCapacity];
        size = 0;
    }

    // O(1) push: store value, track min-so-far, track sum-so-far
    public void push(int value) {
        if (size == stack.length) {
            Node[] newStack = new Node[stack.length * 2];
            System.arraycopy(stack, 0, newStack, 0, size);
            stack = newStack;
        }

        int minBelow = (size == 0) ? value : Math.min(value, stack[size - 1].minBelowOrEqual);
        long sumBelow = (size == 0) ? value : stack[size - 1].sumBelowOrEqual + value;

        stack[size] = new Node(value, minBelow, sumBelow);
        size++;
    }

    // O(1) pop
    public int pop() {
        if (size == 0) throw new EmptyStackException();
        int value = stack[size - 1].value;
        size--;
        return value;
    }

    // O(1) query: return the sum of all elements
    public long getSum() {
        if (size == 0) return 0L;
        return stack[size - 1].sumBelowOrEqual;
    }

    // O(1) bonus: get min in stack
    public int getMin() {
        if (size == 0) throw new EmptyStackException();
        return stack[size - 1].minBelowOrEqual;
    }

    public int getSize() {
        return size;
    }
}
```

**Feature choice: Running sum** — more interesting than min/max (which are classic) because it's less obvious how to make it cheap. By caching cumulative sums at each level, you get O(1) access to total stack sum without scanning. This enables useful queries like "how much total work has been queued" in real systems.

