public class MinTrackingStack {
    private static class Node {
        int value;
        int minAtThisPoint;

        Node(int value, int minAtThisPoint) {
            this.value = value;
            this.minAtThisPoint = minAtThisPoint;
        }
    }

    private Node[] items;
    private int size;
    private static final int INITIAL_CAPACITY = 10;

    public MinTrackingStack() {
        items = new Node[INITIAL_CAPACITY];
        size = 0;
    }

    /**
     * Push O(1)
     */
    public void push(int value) {
        if (size == items.length) {
            resize();
        }
        int currentMin = (size == 0) ? value : Math.min(items[size - 1].minAtThisPoint, value);
        items[size] = new Node(value, currentMin);
        size++;
    }

    /**
     * Pop O(1)
     */
    public int pop() {
        if (size == 0) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Stack underflow");
        }
        return items[--size].value;
    }

    /**
     * Query: return the minimum value currently in the stack O(1)
     */
    public int getMin() {
        if (size == 0) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Stack is empty");
        }
        return items[size - 1].minAtThisPoint;
    }

    private void resize() {
        Node[] newItems = new Node[items.length * 2];
        System.arraycopy(items, 0, newItems, 0, size);
        items = newItems;
    }

    public int peek() {
        if (size == 0) throw new IllegalStateException("Stack is empty");
        return items[size - 1].value;
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return size == 0;
    }
}
```

**Design:** Each stack node caches the minimum value from the bottom of the stack up to that node. On push, we compute the new min in O(1) by comparing the incoming value against the previous top's cached minimum. On pop, we just discard. On `getMin()`, we return the cached minimum from the top node—O(1) with no auxiliary space beyond the one cached int per node.

