public class MinStack {
    private static class Node {
        int value;
        int minSoFar;

        Node(int value, int minSoFar) {
            this.value = value;
            this.minSoFar = minSoFar;
        }
    }

    private Node head;

    /**
     * Push a value onto the stack.
     * Time: O(1), Space: O(1) per operation
     */
    public void push(int value) {
        int newMin = (head == null) ? value : Math.min(value, head.minSoFar);
        head = new Node(value, newMin);
    }

    /**
     * Pop and return the top value.
     * Time: O(1)
     */
    public int pop() {
        if (head == null) throw new IllegalStateException("Stack underflow");
        int value = head.value;
        head = head.next;
        return value;
    }

    /**
     * Query: return the minimum value in the entire stack.
     * Time: O(1)
     */
    public int queryMin() {
        if (head == null) throw new IllegalStateException("Stack is empty");
        return head.minSoFar;
    }
}
```

**Design insight:** Each node caches the minimum value from that point downward. On push, compute `min(newValue, previousMin)` in O(1). On pop, discard the cached minimum along with the node. This gives you a free minimum query by reading a single field.
